{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1511553612,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0Njg4OTkwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-346889903"},"message":"The version you are using has no support for image/picture metadata. This is only in master, which will be released by @yarikoptic at some point.\n\nIn master this works like this:\n\n```\n# enable image metadata parsing in a dataset\ngit config --file .datalad/config --add datalad.metadata.nativetype image\n# run metadata parsers to build compact metadata representation\ndatalad aggregate-metadata\n# query metadata\ndatalad metadata \u003cyourfile\u003e\n```\n\nYou could also use `search` afterwards. We require a dedicated aggregation step, because extraction can be very costly in some cases. ATM there is no incremental aggregation, but this could be added when there is demand.\n\nAdding a new parser is relatively simple. Take a look at the examples: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/tree/master/datalad/metadata/parsers\n\nFill in those functions with your barcode recognition, and edit `__init__.py` to enable your parser.\n\nNote that this is all still a bit fluid. Should you decide to send a PR for a new parser, please make sure to add appropriate tests, so we can include it in any refactoring to may take place in the future.\n\nWriting dedicated docs on how to write parser is already on my TODO.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1512398255,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0ODk4MDE0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-348980140"},"message":"I'm also interested in indexing the content of the files (#1996). Would it work to just extract the text content of the file and add that to the metadata? What kind of schema should I use? I'm not very familiar with json-ld. In case of a custom schema, is it possible to store the schema in the repository as well? Where is the whoosh index stored?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1512468955,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0OTI1ODkwNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-349258904"},"message":"Yes, it is possible to just store the plain text content as metadata. Yes, you can make up any schema you like, and yes, you can store the description of that schema within the dataset itself (actually, that desciption becomes part of the metadata).\n\nAs an example you could check out this parser: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/master/datalad/metadata/parsers/nifti1.py\n\nYou'll see that it defines its own vocabulary at the top, and that this vocabulary is included in the returned dataset-global metadata under the `@context` key. This is pretty much what the JSON-LD context is made for: a description of the keys used in the other parts of the JSON structure.\n\nWhile this is all very flexible, I would strongly advice to do a little research if anyone already came up with a standard for doing something like this (e.g. storing OCR data in a PDF; maybe there is a demo somewhere how to achieve that with XMP (https://www.adobe.com/products/xmp.html), which datalad supports already. If that is the case, I would make sure to be *heavily* inspired by that (choice of key names, links to existing definitions for such keys, choice and format of values,...). In my experience, there is  much to learn from existing solutions to metadata problems...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1512469107,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM0OTI1OTU2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-349259560"},"message":"Re whoosh index location: At the moment, the whoosh index is auto-generated when needed by `search` and is stored under the local repositories `.git/` folder. There is some debate whether to attempt a more portable solution instead, but at the moment we have no clear use case that would justify this effort.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1514815321,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NDY1NDgzMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-354654833"},"message":"How do I add the metadata without a parser in bulk? For the document scanning use case I have `.tiff` files with possibly multiple separate documents inside each file  I then separate the documents and gather the metadata for each document. While it is possible to make this a parser, it is pretty specific to my use case. Adding the metadata separately would also allow for incremental processing.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1514884824,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NDcyNjgyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-354726823"},"message":"I see several angles:\n\n- Everything is pretty specific to someone's use case ;-) I still think that a parser is the most suitable approach.\n- Incremental metadata aggregation is already on the TODO list, and will be done sooner or later (if you are interested in helping out with this we could discuss that separately)\n- Dumping some (expensive to generate) metadata into a dedicated place is something that should be supported by DataLad. However, I would want to avoid inventing needless formats and standards.\n\nMy recommendation for your use case would be this:\n\n- take a look at Adobe XMP as means to store your metadata\n- XMP can be embedded in TIFF file directly (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extensible_Metadata_Platform), so you could process each file separately and directly, without creating additional ones\n- XMP can also be stored in sidecar files, in case you do not want to modify the original files\n- we have a XMP parser prototype (https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/master/datalad/metadata/parsers/xmp.py) -- needs much work and would also need to get the ability to \"see\" sidecar files, but would be a good start\n- this page has the specs on how to structure metadata in XMP: http://www.adobe.com/devnet/xmp.html -- importantly XMP pretty much take any vocabulary, so you can be as standard or free as you desire, without imposing any constraints on the DataLad parser (or vice versa).\n- going with XMP is building on something that is universally known, e.g. any desktop search engine, or Google know how and where to look for XMP metadata","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"668736a0709b6830bb0f976123c8ef651d7cf6f9"},"timestamp":1514901661,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NDc3MjI2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-354772260"},"message":"Indeed, XMP is definitely the way to go. Supposedly git-annex also supports [extracting metadata with exiftool](http://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/automatically_adding_metadata/), but I couldn't get it to work.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1514905009,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NDc4MzU3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-354783579"},"message":"We have a parser for exif too.\n\n\n\nOn Jan 2, 2018 15:01, \"Kusti Skytén\" \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e wrote:\n\n\u003e Indeed, XMP is definitely the way to go. Supposedly git-annex also\n\u003e supports extracting metadata with exiftool\n\u003e \u003chttp://git-annex.branchable.com/tips/automatically_adding_metadata/\u003e,\n\u003e but I couldn't get it to work.\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you commented.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-354772260\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAIVHxIiv_dmstKfxeBZV7CRdKqzlLmFks5tGjaegaJpZM4Qp-Rx\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519105191,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2Njg3MjM0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1994#issuecomment-366872343"},"message":"I am closing this issue for now, as the topic seems to have been exhausted. Please feel free to (re)open another issue, if the need arises.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519105191,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQ4MTcxMzM0MQ=="},"status":2}]}